1
00:00:06,005 --> 00:00:08,274
[engine starts]

2
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:18,833
[Jerry] This is a 1963 
VW Karmann Ghia in sea blue.

3
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:24,524
It happens to be equipped 
with a 4-cylinder,

4
00:00:24,591 --> 00:00:28,028
Porsche 912 engine, suspension and brakes.

5
00:00:28,695 --> 00:00:30,263
But that is a secret.

6
00:00:31,031 --> 00:00:33,933
This was a sports car for guys
that wanted a sports car,

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,468
but also wanted a VW.

8
00:00:36,002 --> 00:00:37,604
So, you had to have a sense of humor.

9
00:00:38,171 --> 00:00:39,305
I love it.

10
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:41,366
[phone ringing]

11
00:00:41,433 --> 00:00:42,433
This is Joel.

12
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:43,566
[Jerry] Joel, what's up?

13
00:00:43,633 --> 00:00:45,566
-Jerry, what's happening?
-Nothing.

14
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:47,500
[Joel] Are you thinking what I'm thinking?

15
00:00:47,566 --> 00:00:48,433
[Jerry] Yes.

16
00:00:53,019 --> 00:00:54,587
Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld,

17
00:00:54,666 --> 00:00:57,800
and this is Comedians
in Cars Getting Coffee.

18
00:00:59,366 --> 00:01:01,000
[Jerry] Today my guest is Joel Hodgson,

19
00:01:01,066 --> 00:01:03,699
creator of Mystery Science Theater 3000,

20
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:06,566
and one of my favorite 
cultural visionaries.

21
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:08,902
[Joel] You know, I knew you
when you only had one car.

22
00:01:09,566 --> 00:01:11,166
That's how long I've known you.

23
00:01:18,111 --> 00:01:20,647
[Jerry] There's Hernandez
Sewer Cleaning &amp; Heating.

24
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:23,500
For all your cleaning needs.

25
00:01:23,566 --> 00:01:25,533
[Jerry] Video inspection of sewer lines.

26
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,566
Well, there's a movie I want to see.

27
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:30,866
"Honey, run that video of our sewer line
one more time."

28
00:01:30,933 --> 00:01:32,399
"What's on cable tonight?"

29
00:01:32,459 --> 00:01:36,062
"Uh, not too much. We got
the video inspection of sewer lines."

30
00:01:36,129 --> 00:01:37,630
[Jerry] "Let's watch that.

31
00:01:37,699 --> 00:01:40,133
And then, show my colonic inspection.
What's that called?"

32
00:01:41,201 --> 00:01:45,572
-[coughs]
-[both laughing]

33
00:01:46,866 --> 00:01:50,100
How do we both go to that, right?
So quickly.

34
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,783
[Jerry] Do you remember
somebody wrote a piece,

35
00:01:56,850 --> 00:02:02,689
and they described you as the adult result
of an overstimulated childhood?

36
00:02:03,333 --> 00:02:05,166
-Do you remember that?
-No, I don't remember.

37
00:02:05,233 --> 00:02:08,033
[Jerry] You took every product
they put out there,

38
00:02:08,094 --> 00:02:10,964
every cereal, right to the soul.

39
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:16,803
A guy who thought Kellogg's and Mattel
had a master plan that couldn't fail.

40
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:21,307
And, no, not in the least.
They were not thinking about me at all.

41
00:02:21,374 --> 00:02:22,942
[cyclist talks indistinctly]

42
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:24,299
You got it.

43
00:02:24,433 --> 00:02:26,900
-Oh, [bleep] you.
-[Jerry] Oh.

44
00:02:26,966 --> 00:02:28,900
I know, I think he wants to ride with us.

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,466
He does. Crack your window.

46
00:02:31,551 --> 00:02:33,853
-[cyclist] I thought that was you...
-[Jerry] I like that bike.

47
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:35,054
[cyclist] I got something for you.

48
00:02:35,155 --> 00:02:36,322
I don't want it.

49
00:02:36,389 --> 00:02:37,624
[cyclist laughs]

50
00:02:37,733 --> 00:02:40,166
Wow! You look good, man.

51
00:02:40,233 --> 00:02:42,166
So do you. You've really kept it up.

52
00:02:48,301 --> 00:02:50,970
Now, is this the kind of car
that you'd really worry about

53
00:02:51,037 --> 00:02:51,971
if I were to wreck it?

54
00:02:52,038 --> 00:02:53,606
Like, you would get upset, right?

55
00:02:55,433 --> 00:02:57,633
Who's not upset when they wreck a car?

56
00:03:00,246 --> 00:03:01,648
[Joel] I guess you're right.

57
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,333
[Jerry] Welcome to North New Jersey,
embroidery capital of the world.

58
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:12,299
I know, I always felt
that should be embroidered.

59
00:03:13,593 --> 00:03:15,295
Like, if they're so great,

60
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:17,430
-show me something big.
-Yeah.

61
00:03:22,866 --> 00:03:25,366
[Jerry] Look at that dump truck.
Now, that's a great vehicle.

62
00:03:25,766 --> 00:03:29,100
That's a thing that's just made
to be shrunken down,

63
00:03:29,166 --> 00:03:30,500
so you can get it in your hand.

64
00:03:31,144 --> 00:03:33,179
People love to re-purpose a phrase.

65
00:03:33,533 --> 00:03:35,833
I mean, "dump truck"
is just sitting there waiting

66
00:03:35,899 --> 00:03:39,866
for some new type of elimination process
to be branded.

67
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:43,122
We're calling it "the dump truck."

68
00:03:43,189 --> 00:03:44,490
[laughing]

69
00:03:44,557 --> 00:03:47,260
And then they look at you, "Good, huh?"

70
00:03:50,266 --> 00:03:51,700
[Jerry] Look at how people drive now.

71
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:55,666
See, improvements in the engineering
of cars has enabled people

72
00:03:55,733 --> 00:03:58,033
to express their inner [bleep] hole-ness.

73
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,399
You think, "Why are people
so ill behaved?" They're not.

74
00:04:01,441 --> 00:04:04,210
They just never had the tools
to express it before.

75
00:04:04,277 --> 00:04:08,348
So technology is making us
bigger [bleep] heads than ever.

76
00:04:08,715 --> 00:04:09,649
That's right.

77
00:04:12,899 --> 00:04:14,333
[Jerry] What do you think of the Cube?

78
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:16,656
Um, I like it.

79
00:04:17,099 --> 00:04:20,866
[Jerry] I think it's, like,
people that want to be driving something,

80
00:04:20,933 --> 00:04:24,033
so when the robots take over,
they won't be mad at them.

81
00:04:24,099 --> 00:04:25,466
-Yeah.
-"I'm one of you."

82
00:04:26,333 --> 00:04:29,466
"Let him pass, he's driving the Cube."

83
00:04:36,209 --> 00:04:38,444
Mad Men is so great still.

84
00:04:38,533 --> 00:04:40,433
-Yeah, I love it.
-I'm still in the middle of it.

85
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:41,566
I'm watching it.

86
00:04:41,633 --> 00:04:44,866
We were on to that in the '80s.

87
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:48,599
That's all we wanted,
was to be advertising guys in the '60s.

88
00:04:48,666 --> 00:04:49,500
That's right.

89
00:04:49,566 --> 00:04:52,000
-We used to talk about that all the time.
-All the time.

90
00:04:52,066 --> 00:04:54,800
-Drinking at lunch...
-Yep.

91
00:04:54,861 --> 00:04:57,330
-...and having a bar in your office.
-Yep.

92
00:04:57,430 --> 00:05:00,066
Is that a shared dream
of all guys our age?

93
00:05:00,133 --> 00:05:01,067
Of course it is.

94
00:05:01,134 --> 00:05:04,370
And the sexual revolution
was all tied up in that.

95
00:05:04,466 --> 00:05:05,300
Yep.

96
00:05:05,338 --> 00:05:07,674
I mean,
that's what the sports cars were about.

97
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:11,411
Women will give this to you,
if you have the right accessories.

98
00:05:19,300 --> 00:05:21,800
Hey, there's a fantastic spot
right up front.

99
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:23,900
How could I say no to that?

100
00:05:31,466 --> 00:05:33,233
Remember when we were kids,
you'd see cars,

101
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:34,566
every one looked so different.

102
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:37,433
Each car looked so different.
So unique unto itself.

103
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:38,900
This whole...

104
00:05:38,966 --> 00:05:40,833
This is just oatmeal.

105
00:05:41,433 --> 00:05:43,966
Yeah, they're grinding
all the edges off, Jerry.

106
00:05:44,033 --> 00:05:46,533
-Just the same thing they're doing to us.
-There's your Saab.

107
00:05:46,599 --> 00:05:48,400
[Jerry chuckles]

108
00:05:59,092 --> 00:06:02,228
-I would love coffee.
-I'll take a coffee, too.

109
00:06:02,295 --> 00:06:03,730
[Jerry] This place is weird.

110
00:06:04,063 --> 00:06:07,633
Most things that are weird
are not weird good.

111
00:06:07,734 --> 00:06:10,269
-And this is weird good.
-This is weird good.

112
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,566
[Jerry] It's another '50s diner.

113
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,700
Why are we looking back all the time?

114
00:06:15,742 --> 00:06:18,745
This diner is about
looking backward, right?

115
00:06:18,811 --> 00:06:20,413
So, why are we looking back?

116
00:06:20,833 --> 00:06:24,000
Because when you look back,
you know what you're gonna say.

117
00:06:24,066 --> 00:06:25,099
That's why.

118
00:06:25,385 --> 00:06:28,087
-Right.
-You know what to say about the past.

119
00:06:28,154 --> 00:06:30,423
But you don't know
what to say about the future.

120
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:32,000
-Damn it, you're smart.
-See?

121
00:06:36,863 --> 00:06:41,000
The economics of a restaurant are always
beyond my mental comprehension.

122
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:46,272
So, I come and I get
a tuna sandwich for $4.50,

123
00:06:46,366 --> 00:06:50,599
and that enables you
to run this whole place?

124
00:06:50,666 --> 00:06:53,433
That's why so many restaurants
don't make it.

125
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:56,400
So many people think they have
a good idea for a restaurant.

126
00:06:56,466 --> 00:06:59,633
And the guy that makes all the money
is the guy who sells the equipment.

127
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:02,733
Just like the guy who sells the guitars
makes the money.

128
00:07:02,799 --> 00:07:04,299
-He does?
-And not the guy in the band.

129
00:07:04,366 --> 00:07:06,633
How many guitars have you bought
over the years?

130
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:08,594
-Two or three.
-Yeah, see?

131
00:07:08,661 --> 00:07:10,062
Do you play the guitar?

132
00:07:10,133 --> 00:07:11,066
-No.
-Yeah.

133
00:07:11,130 --> 00:07:14,400
You know how many I've bought?
Six. And I don't play the guitar.

134
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:20,166
But the other thing
that's an object of fascination

135
00:07:20,206 --> 00:07:22,842
that I've found of comedians of all kinds,

136
00:07:22,909 --> 00:07:27,180
and this goes back to the Mad Men thing,
the corporate environment.

137
00:07:27,246 --> 00:07:31,217
The idea of bosses and employees
is just hilarious to us.

138
00:07:31,584 --> 00:07:33,519
Why is that so funny?

139
00:07:33,853 --> 00:07:36,455
-Well, we don't have to do it, right?
-[laughing]

140
00:07:36,533 --> 00:07:42,433
It's such a typically human attempt
to organize what is un-organizable.

141
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:43,500
-Right?
-You're right.

142
00:07:43,563 --> 00:07:45,531
We just see the hopelessness of trying

143
00:07:45,598 --> 00:07:49,101
to organize human endeavor
into a building.

144
00:07:49,535 --> 00:07:53,272
We'd like to be in a system...

145
00:07:53,339 --> 00:07:54,407
That works.

146
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:59,566
If you can agree
with me that I'm your boss,

147
00:08:00,279 --> 00:08:04,116
and you do what I say,
everything is gonna be fine.

148
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:10,556
What do you think of this ketchup
with the cap on the bottom?

149
00:08:10,623 --> 00:08:11,524
It's great.

150
00:08:11,591 --> 00:08:15,428
How many fights did they have over this
before someone said,

151
00:08:15,495 --> 00:08:19,131
"But the ketchup goes to the bottom.
Put the cap on the bottom."

152
00:08:19,198 --> 00:08:22,835
What about the passing-gas sound problem
of the ketchup?

153
00:08:22,933 --> 00:08:24,366
Do you think they talk about that?

154
00:08:24,837 --> 00:08:25,738
You know...

155
00:08:25,805 --> 00:08:27,039
[squirts]

156
00:08:29,366 --> 00:08:32,100
"Hey, how's that upside-down jar
coming along?"

157
00:08:32,166 --> 00:08:35,299
-"It's fantastic, people love it."
-"Focus groups, off the charts."

158
00:08:35,366 --> 00:08:36,799
"We do have one problem."

159
00:08:36,866 --> 00:08:38,966
"What could possibly be the problem?"

160
00:08:39,018 --> 00:08:40,953
"When the bottle's been
sitting upright..."

161
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:42,989
"Mm-hmm. I'm with you.

162
00:08:43,055 --> 00:08:47,126
It's filled with our good ketchup.
Go ahead."

163
00:08:47,593 --> 00:08:49,996
"Well, sir, it's not something
we've ever faced before

164
00:08:50,066 --> 00:08:51,766
in our ketchup bottles."

165
00:08:51,864 --> 00:08:56,769
"My wife's picking me up in 15 minutes.
Could you get to the point?"

166
00:08:56,836 --> 00:09:00,106
He's gotta be in a tux.
Him and his wife are going to a function.

167
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:04,199
Yeah, he's putting on his cuff links,
he's doing his tie.

168
00:09:04,266 --> 00:09:06,133
Looking at the opera tickets.

169
00:09:09,166 --> 00:09:10,433
"It's this, sir."

170
00:09:14,086 --> 00:09:15,121
"Sounds normal to me.

171
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:19,233
Sounds like our new upside-down bottle
extruding our good ketchup."

172
00:09:19,759 --> 00:09:20,760
[sighs]

173
00:09:22,595 --> 00:09:24,363
"Well, what do you propose we do?

174
00:09:24,430 --> 00:09:28,067
There is a chance that the consumer
will not find it objectionable,

175
00:09:28,133 --> 00:09:32,199
and it'll be kind of like
the slowness of our older bottle."

176
00:09:32,266 --> 00:09:35,366
-"Or?"
-"Or we potentially have a PR problem.

177
00:09:36,175 --> 00:09:39,045
Frankly, we're worried about these
YouTube kids getting a hold of this."

178
00:09:39,111 --> 00:09:42,148
"They could have a field day with this,
if you know what I mean."

179
00:09:43,266 --> 00:09:46,500
"Anderson here feels
if we can get out in front of this thing

180
00:09:46,566 --> 00:09:48,366
and make fun of it ourselves,

181
00:09:48,421 --> 00:09:50,456
then we would be perceived
as in on the joke."

182
00:09:50,523 --> 00:09:52,725
"Roll the 'Who let one?' reel.

183
00:09:52,992 --> 00:09:54,527
This is just a test reel,

184
00:09:54,594 --> 00:09:57,229
but this shows you some
of the funny things we can do with this.

185
00:09:57,296 --> 00:09:59,298
If we get the okay from you, that is."

186
00:09:59,365 --> 00:10:02,635
[laughing]

187
00:10:06,238 --> 00:10:07,640
What are you laughing about?

188
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,400
Why are movies so much more fun
to make fun of than television?

189
00:10:17,466 --> 00:10:21,933
They're aspiring
to present God's point of view.

190
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,233
It's big.

191
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:26,833
This is life in all its fury.

192
00:10:26,892 --> 00:10:28,060
-Yes.
-You know?

193
00:10:28,533 --> 00:10:29,633
[Jerry] Did you like Avatar?

194
00:10:29,695 --> 00:10:32,398
I did. I bought the entire dream.

195
00:10:32,465 --> 00:10:36,035
My favorite part was that guy
in the helicopter with the coffee cup.

196
00:10:37,333 --> 00:10:39,233
When they were attacking. Remember him?

197
00:10:39,672 --> 00:10:42,174
That was part of his breakfast,
killing people.

198
00:10:42,241 --> 00:10:44,076
Part of his complete breakfast.

199
00:10:46,412 --> 00:10:49,682
-Right. Big mug of joe.
-"I just need something.

200
00:10:49,749 --> 00:10:53,219
Why aren't I waking up? Oh, that's right.

201
00:10:53,285 --> 00:10:57,189
I've gotta give someone a taste
of hell from on high."

202
00:10:57,266 --> 00:11:01,500
"Fill these natives full of lead.
That's how I start my day."

203
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:02,728
"That's a bracer."

204
00:11:02,795 --> 00:11:04,697
Uhh. Mmm!

205
00:11:05,333 --> 00:11:06,400
"Now, I'm up."

206
00:11:06,733 --> 00:11:10,166
"If I haven't killed,
I'm just no good in the morning.

207
00:11:10,233 --> 00:11:12,233
"You gonna wanna kill that guy?

208
00:11:12,672 --> 00:11:15,241
Because I could go
for another one right now."

209
00:11:15,333 --> 00:11:17,166
[both laughing]

